<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Canisius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1521">1521</a>–<a href="/years/1597">1597</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Petrus Canisius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Canisius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priest, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a>, who became one of the leading figures of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in the German-speaking lands. His catechisms were reprinted hundreds of times, and he founded or guided Jesuit colleges across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He spent his last years in Fribourg, where he founded the College of Saint Michael in 1582; he was canonised and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-canisius.jpg" alt="Peter Canisius" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Petrus_Canisius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
